From 094efa6b5c9295c9189e2f63256f148c19a1fb76 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 14:35:35 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] Servers: pass the block hash to getrawtransaction for an
 indexed transaction

blockchain.transaction.get forwarded getrawtransaction <txid> <verbose>
with no block hash. For a confirmed transaction bitcoind can answer that
only with -txindex, and a pruned node cannot have -txindex (Core and
Knots refuse the combination), so every confirmed lookup failed against
one.

Fulcrum already knows where each transaction is: txhash2txnum gives its
height, and the header gives the block hash, the lookup behind
blockchain.transaction.get_confirmed_blockhash. Do that lookup in a
worker thread, then pass the hash. bitcoind then needs only the block,
which a proxy such as btc-rpc-proxy (v0.8.0 and later) fetches from
peers when the node has pruned it. An archival node answers it with or
without -txindex, and with -txindex it consults the index first, so this
costs it nothing.

Only for nodes that take the block hash (getRawTransactionTakesBlockHash,
Core, Knots and Litecoin 0.16+); any other node is asked exactly as
before.

A transaction not in the index (unconfirmed, or unknown) is still asked
for without a hash, which finds it in the mempool. When that fails, the
node answers -5 with advice to enable -txindex; replace the message with
one that says the transaction is not in this server's index, keeping the
code and the ElectrumX-style formatting.

The bitcoind request is counted against the client's throttle up front,
on the client thread, as the request is read. Counted only when it
reached bitcoind, after the lookup on the thread pool, the throttle bit
too late: one client could pipeline tens of thousands of lookups (each a
block fetched from peers, on a pruned node) before its reads were
paused, where every other bitcoind-backed method stops at 50.
generic_async_to_bitcoind gains an alreadyCounted flag, and the throttle
block moved, unchanged, into bdThrottleCount. The lookup never throws,
and generic_do_async gains an optional onFail for a job the pool
refuses, so the count is always undone.

The reply is relayed only if it is the transaction asked for (txid of
the raw hex, or the verbose txid): with a block hash it can come from a
block fetched from peers through a proxy.

A lookup that names a block gets the operator's bitcoind_timeout rather
than the fixed 15 s Fulcrum gives every client-originated request: on a
pruned node the block may be fetched from peers, which can take longer
(up to 40 s measured on a live server with Tor and I2P peers).

The block hash comes from BTC::HeaderHashRev, so a block past the
BLAKE2b fork is named by its BLAKE2b id.
